# Rank 1461 / CVE-2026-23950 (GHSA-r6q2-hw4h-h46w): node-tar unpacks entries in
# parallel and keeps that safe with `PathReservations`, a table that hands out
# exclusive access to a path so a symbolic-link entry and a file entry naming
# the same place cannot run at once -- the ordering the unpacker's symlink
# protection depends on. `src/path-reservations.ts` builds the table key by
# lower-casing the path, because the volume may be case-insensitive:
#
#   paths = paths.map(p =>
#     stripTrailingSlashes(join(normalizeUnicode(p))).toLowerCase())
#   ...
#   const q = this.#queues.get(p)
#   if (!q) this.#queues.set(p, [fn])
#
# `String.prototype.toLowerCase` is Unicode's *simple* lowercase mapping: one
# code point in, at most one out. The characters whose lowercase expansion is
# longer than themselves therefore have no lowercase form at all and come back
# unchanged -- the ligature block U+FB00-U+FB06, plus `ss`-class `ß` U+00DF,
# `ẞ` U+1E9E, `ſ` U+017F and `ẛ` U+1E9B. macOS APFS folds with the *full* table
# instead, under which `ﬁ` and `FI` are one file. A tarball carrying a
# `SymbolicLink` entry named `ﬁ` and a `File` entry named `FI` gets two
# reservations, both granted at once, and the file write lands on the link -- an
# arbitrary write outside the extraction directory. The advisory's own
# regression test enumerates exactly that character set.
#
# The fixed revision has two halves. `src/normalize-unicode.ts` turns the canonical form
# into a round trip, `.normalize('NFD').toLocaleLowerCase('en')
# .toLocaleUpperCase('en')`, because `toUpperCase` unlike its counterpart *is* a
# full mapping and is documented to expand these characters (`ﬁ` -> `FI`, `ß` ->
# `SS`); and `reserve()` drops its own `.toLowerCase()`, the fold now being done
# for it.
#
# Step 0: no metadata flag and the pool row is correct. `git show` reads the
# commit, the vulnerable `.toLowerCase()` key and the bare `s.normalize('NFD')`
# canonicalizer are both present at the parent, and both halves of the
# remediation are present at the fixed revision. Recorded CWE-178 is the weakness class;
# the consequence is CWE-59.
#
# Nothing reports this, at either revision, and the reason is a layer boundary
# rather than missing vocabulary. The weakness is a *relation* between three
# nodes -- a path is composed, the composed value is case-folded, and the folded
# value is the key a lookup table is indexed by -- and a `presenceNode` binding
# expresses only co-occurrence of facts inside one function context. Written
# that way the four facts are `call:toLowerCase`, a `node:path` composition call,
# and `call:get` plus `call:set`, none of which is rare and none of which is tied
# to the others. It is built, measured and deliberately not shipped:
#
#   * against the real clone it does discriminate -- one row at 3b1abfae65^ and
#     none at 3b1abfae65, three `--cache off` runs of each revision of the whole
#     tree (18 findings vs 17), the sole row being path-reservations.ts:53,
#     which is `PathReservations.reserve`;
#   * over the reachable set it does not. Exhaustively enumerating every JS/TS
#     file under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ that carries all four needles gives 1277
#     files; scanning the 815 of them under 300 KB (the rest are minified
#     bundles) reports **110 rows in 81 files** for that one true positive.
#     They are ordinary code: an icon-extension check
#     (`ICON_EXTENSIONS.includes(path.extname(file).toLowerCase())`), an
#     Accept-header parser keying a Map by a lower-cased media type, a
#     `new URL(u, base).protocol.toLowerCase()` scheme allowlist, a package-name
#     module index, and 33 rows on minified vendor bundles where the whole file
#     is one function context.
#   * tightening does not rescue it. Restricting the path call to
#     `join`/`basename`/`dirname`/`relative` (dropping `resolve`, which mostly
#     matches `Promise.resolve`) gives 82; additionally requiring a `new Set` in
#     the same body gives 25 -- and that clause is the fixed revision's own layout, not the
#     weakness, since a reservation table need not construct a Set where it
#     builds its keys.
#
# What would close it is the relation, not another token: the binding has to
# know that the value handed to the table's `get`/`set` is the value the fold
# produced. Rank 1360's gap (the JavaScript frontend emits no call-argument
# tokens) is necessary but not sufficient here -- even with `call_arg:` the link
# from `paths = paths.map(p => ...toLowerCase())` to `this.#queues.get(p)` is a
# dataflow fact, not a text fact. Neither `node.context.assign` nor
# `node.context.expr` reaches the fold at all on this file (both measured at 0),
# because the assignment's right-hand side is an arrow function and the fold is
# a member call rather than a binary expression.
#
# Concepts considered for reuse, all rejected: `RecursionGuardIndexedByUnnormalizedKey`
# (a table indexed by the raw key while dispatch used the lower-cased one -- the
# mismatch is between two of the program's own spellings, not between the program
# and the filesystem), `UrlPathParameterCaseNormalizationBypass` (names stored
# under caller-controlled case, i.e. no fold at all),
# `CaseInsensitiveLocalIdentityAuthorizationBypass` and
# `ComrakDangerousUrlCaseSensitiveGrammar` (both the inverse defect, a
# case-*sensitive* comparison letting variants through), and
# `UncanonicalizedPathContainmentCheck` (a containment test, not an identity
# key). None judges a fold weaker than the filesystem's.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1461: a path reservation table lower-cases the joined entry path to build its key"
  lang javascript
  file src/path-reservations.ts
  reject VYQL-PATH-062
  code
  ```
  import { join } from 'node:path'
  import { normalizeUnicode } from './normalize-unicode.js'
  import { stripTrailingSlashes } from './strip-trailing-slashes.js'

  export class PathReservations {
    #queues = new Map()
    #reservations = new Map()

    reserve(paths, fn) {
      paths = paths.map(p => {
        return stripTrailingSlashes(join(normalizeUnicode(p))).toLowerCase()
      })
      const dirs = new Set(paths)
      this.#reservations.set(fn, { dirs, paths })
      for (const p of paths) {
        const q = this.#queues.get(p)
        if (!q) {
          this.#queues.set(p, [fn])
        } else {
          q.push(fn)
        }
      }
      return true
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1461: fix folds case in the unicode normalizer and the key builder stops lower-casing"
  lang javascript
  file src/path-reservations.ts
  reject VYQL-PATH-062
  code
  ```
  import { join } from 'node:path'
  import { normalizeUnicode } from './normalize-unicode.js'
  import { stripTrailingSlashes } from './strip-trailing-slashes.js'

  export class PathReservations {
    #queues = new Map()
    #reservations = new Map()

    reserve(paths, fn) {
      paths = paths.map(p => {
        return stripTrailingSlashes(join(normalizeUnicode(p)))
      })
      const dirs = new Set(paths)
      this.#reservations.set(fn, { dirs, paths })
      for (const p of paths) {
        const q = this.#queues.get(p)
        if (!q) {
          this.#queues.set(p, [fn])
        } else {
          q.push(fn)
        }
      }
      return true
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1461: the unicode canonicalizer memoizes an NFD form that is not a case fold"
  lang javascript
  file src/normalize-unicode.ts
  reject VYQL-PATH-062
  code
  ```
  const normalizeCache = Object.create(null)

  const MAX = 10000
  const cache = new Set()

  export const normalizeUnicode = s => {
    if (!cache.has(s)) {
      normalizeCache[s] = s.normalize('NFD')
    } else {
      cache.delete(s)
    }
    cache.add(s)
    return normalizeCache[s]
  }
  ```

test "rank1461: fix makes the canonical form a full case-fold round trip"
  lang javascript
  file src/normalize-unicode.ts
  reject VYQL-PATH-062
  code
  ```
  const normalizeCache = Object.create(null)

  const MAX = 10000
  const cache = new Set()

  export const normalizeUnicode = s => {
    if (!cache.has(s)) {
      normalizeCache[s] = s
        .normalize('NFD')
        .toLocaleLowerCase('en')
        .toLocaleUpperCase('en')
    } else {
      cache.delete(s)
    }
    cache.add(s)
    return normalizeCache[s]
  }
  ```
